  "notes": "The Cheiron producer for the harder, more electronic boy-band records: 86–105 straight, backbeat on 2&4, minor tonic with i–VI–VII cycles and ballad productions in major turning IV–I–V–vi. \"Hard drums\" is the stated aesthetic and it is authored as a velocity tier at 112–127 accent — the loudest on the Cheiron bench — with \"a buzzy electronic layer on top\" as a `perc`/`rim` block. Two placement facts follow: \"kick doubled on the 8th before the backbeat\" (anchors on 1 and \"3&\", secondary \"1&\") and \"metallic 16th tick\", which is the pinned 16th hat. The delta that actually defines him is negative: **a string crescendo rather than a drum fill** is the standard lift into bar 1, so the fills move to every 16 bars and the hat rolls sit at 0.02 — this board does not roll into sections. The bass is \"gated synth bass tight to the kick, root-plus-octave, with a 16th stutter on turnarounds\": `mirror_kick` at 0.93 with the octave as its passing tone. His productions \"treat the group's harmony as the lead instrument\", which is the counter at a high ratio in sustained pad and octave echo. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability, the velocity tier and the melody register — the documented C-sharp2–G-sharp5 span is five voices splitting one line, and 62–92 is this author's single-part reading of it.",
